WEBVTT

00:00:09,230 --> 00:00:13,230
I'm considering waiting to start chemotherapy.

00:00:13,230 --> 00:00:19,230
I ask my ecologist if she could order a CT scan in a month or two to see if there are any changes either way.

00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:21,230
I've not heard her answer yet.

00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:30,230
My question for you is, is it unrealistic to expect this protocol to show some improvement in one or two months?

00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:38,230
I know with God's help, anything is possible, just trying to get a feeling for your experience with people on these protocols.

00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:43,230
Okay, so it really depends on a couple of things.

00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:54,230
Well, first of all, to come into the, I'm considering the way to start chemo, I asked my ecologist if she could order a CT scan in a month or two to see if there's any changes either way.

00:00:54,230 --> 00:01:01,230
Most likely the answer to that question from your ecologist is going to be no.

00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:14,230
Because there are some qualifications to be considered in whether your insurance will pay for another CT scan.

00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:22,230
If you're not doing the therapy that is recommended, most likely your insurance will not approve another CT scan.

00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:25,230
So that's probably not an option.

00:01:25,230 --> 00:01:33,230
That's probably going to be what the ecologist would say plus the ecologist would have a bias in that.

00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:37,230
Well, if you're not going to do what I'm telling you to do, I'm not going to order another CT scan.

00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:47,230
They may be totally okay with ordering blood work for you, as long as your comment to them was, I may want to wait on the chemo.

00:01:47,230 --> 00:01:50,230
Could I continue to get blood work while I'm in the decision making process?

00:01:50,230 --> 00:01:53,230
I'm not saying no, but I'm just postponing this.

00:01:53,230 --> 00:01:58,230
They may be open to continue to do blood work and to see you and do exams and such.

00:01:58,230 --> 00:02:12,230
But I don't know about getting an expensive test like a CT scan, whether they'd be open to that, and I'd highly doubt if your insurance would be open to that.

00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:21,230
Is it unrealistic to expect that any nutritional rife, any alternative protocol would show improvement in one or two months?

00:02:21,230 --> 00:02:24,230
No, it's not unrealistic.

00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:26,230
Is it probable?

00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:30,230
Maybe it partly depends on the rate that the cancer is growing.

00:02:30,230 --> 00:02:45,230
And if you just were diagnosed with cancer and let's say it's staged for, it's metastasized to multiple organs, we don't know the rate that need the speed of growth.

00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:53,230
So if it's growing very rapidly, sometimes you need to do some chemo to knock things down.

00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:55,230
It would be a little different story.

00:02:55,230 --> 00:03:05,230
If a person, and I don't remember who asked this question, what their cancer is, but let's pretend it's a couple scenarios so we could get a better example.

00:03:05,230 --> 00:03:17,230
If a woman had a lump in her breast and she palpated that lump and felt there was something wrong and finally went in after several months, this is not getting any better.

00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:25,230
I went through a diagnostic procedure and she was diagnosed with breast cancer and the lump was the size of a grape.

00:03:25,230 --> 00:03:34,230
And the oncologist told her we need to do chemotherapy right away to shrink it and then we're going to do surgery that's typical scenario with a lump in the breast.

00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:43,230
And the woman said, is it unrealistic for me to do, you know, a natural protocol, whatever that consists of.

00:03:43,230 --> 00:03:54,230
If I can prove it in one or two months, I would say that's not unrealistic because we know the cancer is so far just contained in one spot.

00:03:54,230 --> 00:04:06,230
You just noticed it, you know, six months ago, you have a lump, whatever size it is, and it hasn't changed dramatically over the time that you've noticed it.

00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:16,230
Is it realistic to give it 30 to 60 days to see if we can stop the growth of it? I think that's very realistic.

00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:29,230
But if a different scenario was the woman had a lump in her breast, she went in and had a biopsy, it was positive for cancer and she had an MRI and she had metastasis to the lungs, metastasis to the bone.

00:04:29,230 --> 00:04:39,230
And is that going to be the same scenario? Well, no, because it's further down this down stage.

00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:49,230
And is it unrealistic to see, is it unrealistic to expect that the nutritional protocol is going to see improvement?

00:04:49,230 --> 00:04:54,230
I don't, I wouldn't say it's not realistic. Everything's possible.

00:04:54,230 --> 00:05:01,230
We've seen miraculous changes in people, but the probability is lower.

00:05:01,230 --> 00:05:08,230
So if a person is in a later stage cancer, the probability goes down.

00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:21,230
Now, again, this is so dependent on every case. If the person was given a chemotherapy regimen that was basically not curative,

00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:28,230
meaning we're going to just do chemo because that's all we got to do, your stage four, we don't have anything else.

00:05:28,230 --> 00:05:37,230
If that's really what's being offered, then I would say that it's not that you're necessarily expecting giant changes,

00:05:37,230 --> 00:05:47,230
but you're, you're really trying to have a protocol that's going to give you a much longer life and hopefully be curative, but have no side effects.

00:05:47,230 --> 00:05:59,230
I don't see any problem with, with postponing things as long as you stay on top of, you know, subjective findings,

00:05:59,230 --> 00:06:06,230
as my pain gotten worse, objective findings. Can I monitor this with different blood work?

00:06:06,230 --> 00:06:11,230
You could get lots of clues in blood work, whether your cancer is growing or not.

00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:25,230
Let's say you look at a CBC with differential, if you have an increase prevalence of anemia is going to be assigned that the cancer is growing.

00:06:25,230 --> 00:06:33,230
That's a possibility. It increases in your alkaline phosphatase. It increases in your calcium. That's all signs that your cancer is growing.

00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:44,230
If you have positive tumor marker tests that are showing up and you do a subsequent test in two weeks and you're doing blood work every two weeks,

00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:51,230
and your tumor markers are double lead, that's a sign that we need to use something else to try to knock this down.

00:06:51,230 --> 00:07:00,230
The alternative perspective approach is not, you know, stealthful enough to knock this down on its own.

00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:08,230
So there are ways to monitor it, short of getting a CT stand, which probably won't be approved.

00:07:08,230 --> 00:07:17,230
So you can, you know, do blood work at a very regular basis. I've had people start when we're doing blood work weekly.

00:07:17,230 --> 00:07:25,230
So we want to keep a close eye on this and they're willing, you know, even if their oncologists won't order that, we can order that blood work.

00:07:25,230 --> 00:07:32,230
And, you know, it's not super expensive, like a $5,000 CT scan.

00:07:32,230 --> 00:07:39,230
And we can order that and keep an eye on it and make sure we're not going severely in the wrong direction.

00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:44,230
Either way, I would always be super kind to my oncologists, be very thankful to them.

00:07:44,230 --> 00:07:50,230
Even if you prayed about this and prayed about this and you don't feel peace about moving forward with chemotherapy,

00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:59,230
I would still tell them that exactly what is going on honestly and praying about this and don't feel peace about moving forward.

00:07:59,230 --> 00:08:02,230
It doesn't mean I'm saying no, I'm just saying no for right now.

00:08:02,230 --> 00:08:06,230
I really appreciate you and I want to keep a relationship with you.

00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:10,230
That would be my recommendation and how you talk to a oncologist.

00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:19,230
If they get angry and they, you know, they're the my way or the highway type personality, then just go to a different oncologist.

00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:32,230
You'll find somebody who will work with you however you want to be worked with and understands your case and your dilemma and your spirituality in this and your decision making process.

00:08:32,230 --> 00:08:34,230
So we just want to keep an eye on it.

00:08:34,230 --> 00:08:43,230
So if a person comes to me and they have stage 4 cancer, they're like, I don't want to do chemo, then we do want to keep an eye on things close more closely.

00:08:43,230 --> 00:08:45,230
Hope that helps.

